On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:39:07 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> This whole interface is quite nice and basically exactly what I want.

Thanks. v4 keeps it as it was, except in one place: daemon(8) cannot
stop on a failed run, so that part changed, as described below.

>> -	    PATH="${root}/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin" \
>> +	    PATH="${localbase}/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin" \
>
> This shouldn't be baked in... if we need to update the PATH, it should
> come via env var provided by the config file.

Agreed, and fixed. start now passes PATH through as you have it, and
a PATH in lab_process_maild_env replaces it. The prefix was only
there so daemon(8) could find lab-process-mail, and start now gives
daemon(8) the absolute path from bin/ instead. daemon(8) itself is
found through env -P with the caller's PATH, so a PATH in the conf
cannot hide it.

If you would rather the daemon get no PATH at all unless the conf
sets one, that is a one-word change. The cost is that jj, notmuch,
and claude are not found without a conf, since sh's default is
/usr/bin:/bin.

>> +		with_env lab-process-mail &
>
> Maybe I'm misunderstanding but it shouldn't need to background the
> process, should it? That's exactly what daemon(8) does.

You read it right; it did not need to. I had kept the background
child and the wait only so that a SIGTERM would be handled while a
pass or the sleep was still running. That was the old structure
carried over, not something the design needed.

> In any case, the point of introducing daemon(8) is so that the program
> itself can be a one-shot script, but can still be controlled as a
> daemon. Backgrounding a process misses the point.

v4 is posted as "[PATCH v4 0/4] lab-process-mail: Add
lab-process-maild" [1]. lab-process-maild no longer has a run command
or a loop. start runs

    env -i ... daemon -f -H -o LOG -P PIDFILE -R INTERVAL \
        -t lab-process-maild LOCALBASE/bin/lab-process-mail

so daemon(8) does the detaching, the log, the pidfile, and the
rerunning, and the script is start, stop, status, and the conf.

Two duties of the old loop had to move into lab-process-mail, each
as its own patch:

- Stopping on a stuck message. daemon(8) reruns the child after any
  exit, and I checked on 15.1 that this includes a failed one. Instead,
  lab-process-mail records a stuck message, and later runs fail
  before the agent starts while it is still the oldest in the inbox.
  That costs a fetch, not a pass, and sends no repeat notice. Every
  other failure is retried after one interval, which fixes the
  transient case in "[BUG] lab-process-mail gets stuck" [2]. The
  silent-failure part of that report is still open.
- Finishing the pass on stop. daemon(8) forwards SIGTERM to its child,
  and lab-process-mail now traps it and exits once its pass is done.
  I checked that daemon(8) waits for that, and does not restart the
  child.

One cost to note is that lab-process-mail answers one message per run
and daemon(8) waits the full interval after every run. A batch of ten
messages therefore takes nine extra minutes at the default of 60. A
smaller lab_process_maild_interval shortens that, since a run on an
empty inbox costs only a fetch.

